<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Meloria (1284)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1284">1284</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Meloria was a naval battle fought on 6 August 1284 near the islet of Meloria off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>. The Genoese fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-doria">Oberto Doria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetto-zaccaria">Benedetto Zaccaria</a> destroyed the Pisan fleet and took thousands of prisoners, many of whom died in captivity. The defeat ended Pisa's power as a maritime republic and left Genoa dominant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-meloria-1284.jpg" alt="Battle of Meloria (1284)" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:How_the_Genoese_discomfited_the_Pisans_at_Meloria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 13th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> founded the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and its alliance with the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a>, which granted it most of its free trading rights, increased Genoa's wealth and power while decreasing Venetian and Pisan commerce. In 1282 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> tried to take control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> when Sinucello, judge of Cinarca, rebelled against Genoa and asked for Pisan help. A Genoese squadron blockaded Pisan trade near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> that August, and in 1283 both sides prepared for war. Pisa gathered soldiers from Tuscany, while Genoa built 120 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, sixty owned by the Republic and the rest rented out, needing at least 15,000 to 17,000 rowers and seamen.</p><p>Early in 1284 part of the Genoese merchant fleet defeated a Pisan force on its way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. In the same year the Genoese attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-torres">Porto Torres</a> and Sassari in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and blockaded Porto Pisano, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetto-zaccaria">Benedetto Zaccaria</a> took thirty ships to support the siege of Sassari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Pisan fleet, which carried members of every leading family and most officers of state, came out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> in a single body under the Podestà Morosini and his lieutenants Ugolino della Gherardesca and Andreotto Saraceno. According to Agostino Giustiniani, the first Genoese line had fifty-eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and eight panfili, with the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-doria">Oberto Doria</a> in the centre, the Spinola galleys on the right and the Doria galleys on the left. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaccaria">Zaccaria</a>'s twenty galleys stood so far behind that the Pisans could not tell whether they were warships or tenders. The Pisans fought the first line by ramming and boarding until Zaccaria's squadron fell on their flank. Their fleet was nearly annihilated, the Podestà was captured and Ugolino fled with a few vessels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, attacked also by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and Lucca, never recovered. Two years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> took Porto Pisano, the city's access to the sea, and filled up the harbour, and in 1406 Florence conquered Pisa. Count Ugolino was later starved to death with several of his sons and grandsons, a fate recounted in the 33rd canto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s Inferno. Among the prisoners was Rustichello da Pisa, who co-wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>'s account of his travels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Meloria (1284)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its long rivalry with Pisa over Corsica, Sardinia and trade ended at the <b>Battle of Meloria</b> in 1284, when Oberto Doria destroyed the Pisan fleet and took thousands of prisoners, crippling Pisa as a naval power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Genoa destroyed the Pisan fleet at <b>Meloria</b> in 1284, and the Kingdom of Aragon took Sardinia in 1324.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-pisa">Republic of Pisa</a></li></ul></section>
